# AIPL's own syntax, as a `Grammar` value — the third and largest consumer of
# the parser library, and the one the whole thing was built for. It is the only
# description of the language there is: every compile runs this grammar over
# `lex_aipl.aipl`'s token stream, the compiler's own sources included.
#
# **It is a PEG, and that shapes it.** The driver has ordered choice and no
# left recursion, so three patterns carry most of the grammar:
#
#   - *List rules* (`param_list = param | param_list COMMA param` in a
#     left-recursive grammar) are one `Many` with a separator. What varies
#     between them is whether the list may be empty and whether a trailing
#     separator is allowed, and those are `min` and `trailing_sep` — two
#     arguments in place of the wrapper productions each list would otherwise
#     spell out by hand.
#   - *Postfix chains* (`postfix`, `base_ty`) are an atom followed by
#     `Many(suffix)`, rather than a rule recursing on its own left edge.
#   - *`expr binop expr`* is `Climb`, whose levels are `op_precedence`'s, `..`
#     included (level 4, between `==` and `<`).
#
# What ordered choice does not do for free is pick the right alternative: it
# commits to the first that matches, so every choice here is written
# longest-first — `IDENT "(" args ")"` before `IDENT "{" fields "}"` before a
# bare `IDENT`, `let (a, b) = ..` before `let x = ..`. Reading a choice from the
# top is reading it in the order the parser tries it.
#
# Two shapes are right-recursive on purpose:
#
#   - `block_body`/`block_tail`. Written as `Many(stmt)` followed by an
#     optional trailing expression, a PEG parses the trailing expression
#     *twice* — once inside the statement alternative that then fails on its
#     missing `;` — and that doubling compounds through nested blocks. Deciding
#     after the expression parses it once.
#   - `loop_body` likewise. A loop body admits no trailing expression at all,
#     which is why it is a separate rule and not `block` with a flag.
#
# Every production lowers to the AST declared in `ast.aipl`, so this file
# answers three questions rather than two: *what parses*, *the shape of the
# tree*, and *what it means*. The first two are what the `.test` blocks and the
# whole-corpus losslessness check verify; the third is what `lower_aipl_ty`,
# `lower_aipl_pattern`, `lower_aipl_expr`, `lower_aipl_block` and
# `lower_aipl_program` assert, each entering the grammar at the production its
# slice is about.
#
# What is left of stage 5 is the bridge on the other side: a `Program` built
# here still cannot cross the FFI, so `aipl_lower_program` hands back a
# rendering for the Rust-side test to read.
#
# The rules are written in the library's own vocabulary — `Then`, `OneOf`,
# `Many`, `Maybe`, `Term`, `Named`, and `Literal` under the
# conventional `Lit` alias — so a reader who knows `grammar.aipl` knows this
# file. Each rule is an *annotated* binding (`let x: Rule<AiplTok> = ..`) for
# the reason `grammar.aipl` documents: `Literal` and `Named` mention no `K`, so
# a rule made only of them has nothing to pin the type parameter, and the
# annotation is what every constructor inside resolves from.

